Original commit body from 1866c33743cda7d8694938dbce3ade21c44f2c1d:

The Python and JS/TS def/use extractors recorded a binding nested inside an
expression -- a walrus in a condition, `while ((m = re.exec(s)) !== null)`,
the inner target of `x = y = e`, `const a = (b = e)`, `i++` -- as a USE and
never as a definition, so the reaching-def solver had nothing to chain from:
`if (t := os.environ["K"]): sock.sendall(t)` read ddg_mixed / not_attempted
where the same flow as two statements read ddg / confirmed. Java and C already
defined such targets.

Both extractors now walk every expression with one collector that owns the
rule (statement-level assignments go through it too), with three refinements
the Java/C walkers do not make:

- CONTAINED ONLY. A nested binding defines its target only when its value
  goes to a test or to another binding on the same statement. Passed to a
  call, used as a receiver, returned or stored in a container, it stays a use:
  the s3a walk seeds on the source line's definitions and never examines that
  line itself, so defining `k` in `acc.push(k = process.env.K)` let the walk
  exhaust on `console.log(k.length)` and REMOVE the flow through `acc`
  (measured: violated -> confirmed_with_caveats). Filed as its own item.
- CONDITIONAL = WEAK. A binding under `and`/`or`/`&&`/`||`/`??`, a ternary
  branch, a comprehension, an optional call chain or `??=` is a define AND a
  use, so the earlier value still reaches what follows when it is skipped.
- A binding in a closure (lambda, arrow, nested function, class body) stays a
  use. A NESTED field write (`x = this.b = v`) mutates its receiver: weak, the
  receiver read. Statement-level `this.a = v` / `self.a = v` are unchanged.

Not changed: the CfgBuilder, loop/if-header INIT clauses (WI-losod), the Java,
C, Rust and Go extractors (residuals filed).

Addresses WI-tozar-lorav-lusuf-rogak-joras-tivoz-runuj-ritat.

